<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Loombre :: /Library/LaunchAgents/com.loombre.menubar.plist

  LaunchAGENT (per-user GUI domain), the deliberate counterpart to the
  three LaunchDAEMONs next door in installers/macos/pkg/launchd/. The
  daemons run the stack headlessly as `_loombre`; this agent runs the
  menubar controller as whoever is logged in, because a menu bar item
  only exists inside a GUI session and must run as that session's user.

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (rc.1 field report: "the install will be
  successful but nothing happens nor opens"): the pkg installed a fully
  working server, worker, web UI and embedded PostgreSQL — all verified
  running — and then put NOTHING on screen. Loombre.app was laid down in
  /Applications with no autostart of any kind: no login item, no agent.
  The operator saw a finished progress bar and an empty menu bar, with no
  way to tell a healthy install from a dead one. Windows has had the
  equivalent autostart since day one (installers/windows/msi/
  Shortcuts.wxs's HKLM Run key); macOS simply shipped without its peer.

  IN /Library/LaunchAgents (system-wide), not ~/Library/LaunchAgents: the
  install is per-machine (rootVolumeOnly, enable_localSystem — see
  Distribution.xml.tmpl) and runs as root, so it has no business writing
  into one particular user's home. A plist here applies to EVERY user who
  logs in, which matches the daemons' all-users posture and means a
  second account on the same Mac gets the menubar too.

  KeepAlive is deliberately ABSENT (i.e. false). RunAtLoad starts the app
  at login and at install time (postinstall bootstraps it into the console
  user's gui/<uid> domain); if the operator then chooses "Quit" from the
  menu, it STAYS quit until the next login. A KeepAlive'd menu bar item
  that respawns itself the instant you dismiss it is hostile — and unlike
  the daemons, nothing depends on this process: the server, worker and web
  UI keep serving with the menubar closed. It is a viewer, not a component.

  LSUIElement=true in the app's own Info.plist (build-pkg.mjs
  assembleAppBundle) keeps it out of the Dock and Cmd-Tab, so launching it
  here does not steal focus or clutter the session.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.loombre.menubar</string>

  <!-- The executable INSIDE the bundle, not `open -a Loombre`: launchd
       needs a process it can actually own and track. `open` would fork
       the app off into LaunchServices and exit immediately, making this
       agent look like it crash-loops on every login. -->
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/Applications/Loombre.app/Contents/MacOS/Loombre</string>
  </array>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- Agents inherit the logged-in user; no UserName key (that is a
       LaunchDaemon-only concept and launchd rejects it in the GUI
       domain). -->

  <key>ProcessType</key>
  <string>Interactive</string>

  <!-- NO StandardOutPath/StandardErrorPath, deliberately. The obvious
       choice — /Library/Logs/Loombre/ alongside the daemons — is a trap:
       that directory is _loombre-owned 0755, and this agent runs as the
       LOGGED-IN user, who cannot create files in it. launchd treats a
       redirect it cannot open as a spawn failure, so a "helpful" log path
       here would resurrect the exact symptom this agent was added to fix
       (nothing appears in the menu bar). The app's own output goes to the
       unified log instead:
         log stream --predicate 'process == "Loombre"' --level debug
       No per-user log directory is invented either — writing into one
       specific user's home from a per-machine, root-run installer is the
       same overreach this file's /Library placement avoids. -->
</dict>
</plist>
